WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:10.100
The Royal Family, Prime Minister Mackenzie King of Canada, Winston Churchill, and a crowd

00:10.100 --> 00:16.020
of two million review the greatest parade of victorious arms London has ever witnessed.

00:16.020 --> 00:20.060
England's rulers, with the Queen Mother and the two princesses, who set a noble example

00:20.060 --> 00:25.340
for the entire empire during the darkest hours of the war, see the hard-won victory celebrated.

00:25.340 --> 00:40.320
Britain's great war leader typifies the bulldog spirit that made her people hang on grimly

00:40.320 --> 00:42.640
until the enemy fell in defeat.

00:42.640 --> 00:47.200
Yanks too join in the big celebration, proudly parading old glory.

00:47.200 --> 01:02.080
While it's mainly Britain's day, most of the allies are represented.

01:02.080 --> 01:06.440
Coming from all parts of the empire, many waited along the route through two nights,

01:06.440 --> 01:09.200
sleeping in the parks or sitting on curbs.

01:09.200 --> 01:24.800
The rain fell on the big day, but failed entirely to dampen the victory spirit.

01:24.800 --> 01:29.080
Britain's brave daughters, who played such an heroic part, share in the tribute of a

01:29.080 --> 01:42.920
thankful nation resplendent in victory.

01:42.920 --> 01:47.480
At the War Department in Washington, the loot of one of history's most fantastic jewel robberies

01:47.480 --> 01:48.920
is on display.

01:48.920 --> 01:53.240
Three million dollars is the value placed on the crown jewels of Hess, Germany, by members

01:53.240 --> 01:56.720
of the provo marshal's office, who recovered the gems.

01:56.720 --> 02:01.200
Helen Miller tells of the arrest of a colonel, his whack wife, and a major in connection

02:01.200 --> 02:02.440
with the theft.

02:02.440 --> 02:11.560
Almost half of the treasure was discovered in a Chicago baggage locker.

02:11.560 --> 02:14.480
This solitaire is worth 15,000.

02:14.480 --> 02:18.040
Although some of the jewels have been torn from their mountings, many of the royal trinkets

02:18.040 --> 02:26.040
stolen from Cromberg Castle were smuggled into the country in their original form.

02:26.040 --> 02:31.400
One of the prizes of the collection is this $50,000 bracelet, a criminal aftermath of

02:31.400 --> 02:56.400
war.

